# Literate Programming

**Literate programming** is a programming paradigm introduced by Donald Knuth. A
program is written in a natural language with snippets of code interspersed.
From this text usable source code is generated, along with well formatted
human-readable document.

The most important influence for this **literate programming** extension is the
[PBR Book](https://pbr-book.org).

This extension provides a set of tools that help the programmer writing literate
programs. Through automation the process of writing literate programs should be
as painless as possible. The programmer writes his literate programs using
Markdown. When a literate programmer needs a snippet they can add a code fence.
In this extension snippets are called code fragments. Building on the framework
provided by Visual Studio Code this extension introduces code completion, code
actions, definition provider, hover tooltips and a fragment explorer.

The approach for this extension is based on Markdown documents. For this
extension the Markdown specification is only slightly adapted to make supporting
**literate programming** easy. The code fragments are expressed in code fences
as per the Markdown specification, either with surrounding triple backticks or
triple tildes. Along with the programming language identifier the opening line
has been extended to contain the fragment name and type essentially as options
to the code fence. The opening line thus will look like `py : <<fragment name>>`
to create a new fragment, or like `py : <<fragment name>>=+` to amend an
existing fragment.

The order of declaration of code fragments does not matter. It is possible to
reference code fragments before they are created. It is thus possible to write
fragments that reference code fragments that haven't been seen yet. The only
requirement is that code fragments referenced by other code fragments eventually
_are_ created.

## From fragment to source code

Fragments themselves don't directly create source files in most cases, but in
the end source files is what is wanted from this extension.

To create actual source files a fragment creation line needs to be used with a
slightly extended form of the creation tag mentioned above. The name has to be
suffixed with the string `.*` between the chevrons. Furthermore a file name
needs to be specified after the equal sign which is followed by whitespace and a
dollar sign `$`. This is essentially a relative path that is going to be
appended to each workspace folder as the root. A top-level fragment looks like
`py : <<top-level fragment.*>> = ./src/source.py $`. The name of this fragment is
`top-level fragment`, and once it has been fully extrapolated will it be written
to a file in the workspace folder under `src` as the file `source.py`.

The **Literate Programming** extension allows the program author to write
multiple projects in the same Visual Studio Code workspace. Each workspace
folder is the root for its own literate project. Within each project there can
be one or more **literate** files. These files carry the extension `.literate`.
One literate file can contain zero or more code fragments. A literate file can
also contain more than one top level fragment. In other words an author can
create multiple source files within just one **literate** document.

This text describes the **Literate Programming** extension as a **literate**
program.

## Two ways to generate the same program

The editor and CLI are adapters around one engine. They differ in where they
read text and how they report results, not in the fragment language they accept.
The editor's Markdown preview is a separate presentation path: it does not write
source files or the generated book.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Author[Edit literate documents] --> Editor[VS Code adapter]
    Author --> Saved[Save documents to disk]
    Saved --> CLI[Command-line adapter]
    Editor --> Buffers[Snapshot open buffers and disk]
    Buffers --> Engine[Shared generation engine]
    CLI --> Engine
    Engine --> Plan[Diagnostics and planned files]
    Plan --> Gate{Valid project and writes requested?}
    Gate -->|Yes| Safety[Preflight all output destinations]
    Safety --> Write[Write changed source and HTML]
    Gate -->|No| Report[Report results without writing]
    Editor --> Preview[Markdown preview]
    Preview --> Diagrams[Local Mermaid rendering]
```

## Fragment Model

The tools provided by the **Literate Programming** extension are built around
one repository of the project providing all necessary information around
fragments.

The fragment repository handles parsing of **literate** documents, reacting to
changes made by users. The repository provides all fragments found in the
projects added to the current workspace. Additionally the repository will write
out source files and rendered HTML files.

The fragment model is defined in the `FragmentRepository` class, which will be
described in detail after introducing a couple of classes that help the
repository.

Each workspace URI identifies a separate set of state. The diagram shows the
relationships behind navigation: fragment information holds the unexpanded code
and its fence tokens, while grabbed states retain the parsed documents. A
fragment assembled from several documents can have tokens from several states;
its `env` points to the first accepted part's document.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    class FragmentRepository
    class FragmentMap
    class FragmentInformation
    class GrabbedStateList
    class GrabbedState
    class Token
    FragmentRepository "1" --> "0..*" FragmentMap : per workspace URI
    FragmentRepository "1" --> "0..*" GrabbedStateList : per workspace URI
    FragmentMap "1" --> "0..*" FragmentInformation : indexed by name
    GrabbedStateList "1" --> "0..*" GrabbedState : parsed documents
    GrabbedState "1" --> "0..*" Token : Markdown tokens
    FragmentInformation "1" --> "1..*" Token : accepted fence parts
    FragmentInformation "1" --> "1" GrabbedState : first part environment
```

### FragmentMap class

The `FragmentMap` class holds a map of strings, which are the fragment names,
and their associated `FragmentInformation` instances. This map is available
through the `map` property. The class provides also a `clear` method and a
`dispose` method.

``` ts : <<fragment map>>=
class FragmentMap {
  map : Map<string, FragmentInformation>;

  constructor()
  {
    this.map = new Map<string, FragmentInformation>();
  }

```

Clearing retains the map object, so holders of the map see its contents emptied.

``` ts : <<fragment map>>=+
  clear()
  {
    this.map.clear();
  }

```

The map owns no external resources; disposal has the same effect on its contents
as an explicit clear.

``` ts : <<fragment map>>=+
  dispose()
  {
    this.map.clear();
  }
};
```

### List of GrabbedState

The class `GrabbedStateList` holds an array of `GrabbedState` accessible through
the `list` property. The class provides `clear` and `dispose` properties.

`GrabbedState` is collected from the *MarkdownIt* parser. It contains tokens and
related information generated by the parser.


``` ts : <<list of grabbed states>>=
class GrabbedStateList {
  list : Array<GrabbedState>;

  constructor()
  {
    this.list = new Array<GrabbedState>();
  }

```

A refresh replaces the list rather than mutating the previous array. Consumers
holding an earlier array therefore keep that snapshot.

``` ts : <<list of grabbed states>>=+
  clear()
  {
    this.list = new Array<GrabbedState>();
  }

```

Disposal instead drains the current array, releasing the retained parser states
through that same array object.

``` ts : <<list of grabbed states>>=+
  dispose()
  {
    while(this.list.length>0)
    {
      this.list.pop();
    }
  }
};
```

### The FragmentRepository class

The `FragmentRepository` uses several helper classes that we looked at just
above.These we introduce right before defining the repository class.

``` ts : <<fragment repository>>=
<<fragment map>>
<<list of grabbed states>>
<<fragment tag location>>

export class FragmentRepository {
  <<fragment repository member variables>>
  <<fragment repository constructor>>
  <<fragment generation method>>

  <<method to get fragments from repository>>

  <<method to get fragment on line for position>>
  <<method to get token at position>>
  <<method to get state for workspace>>
  <<method to get state for document>>
  <<method to get all reference locations>>

  <<dispose fragment repository>>
}
```

Disposal first closes the input side: pending timers and subscriptions must not
schedule more work while workspace snapshots are being released. The disposed
flag also makes already-queued processing requests inert.

``` ts : <<dispose fragment repository>>=
dispose() {
  this.disposed = true;
  for (const timer of this.inputTimers.values()) { clearTimeout(timer); }
  this.inputTimers.clear();
  this.generatedOutputs.clear();
  for (const subscription of this.inputSubscriptions) { subscription.dispose(); }
  this.inputSubscriptions.length = 0;
  for(let fragmentMap of this.fragmentsForWorkspaceFolders.values())
  {
    fragmentMap.dispose();
  }
  this.fragmentsForWorkspaceFolders.clear();
  for(let grabbedState of this.grabbedStateForWorkspaceFolders.values())
  {
    grabbedState.dispose();
  }
  this.grabbedStateForWorkspaceFolders.clear();
      this.sourceDocuments.clear();
      this.previewThemes.clear();
      this.occurrences.clear();
}
```

#### Member variables

Our `FragmentRepository` needs a couple of member variables to function
properly. We'll need an instance of a properly configured *MarkdownIt* parser.

``` ts : <<fragment repository member variables>>=
private md : MarkdownIt;
private processingQueue: Promise<void> = Promise.resolve();
private sourceDocuments = new Map<string, SourceDocument[]>();
private previewThemes = new Map<string, string>();
private occurrences = new Map<string, FragmentOccurrence[]>();
private disposed = false;
private inputTimers = new Map<string, ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>>();
private inputSubscriptions: vscode.Disposable[] = [];
private generatedOutputs = new Map<string, Set<string>>();
```

Filter input events before queueing work. Generated destinations are remembered
per workspace, including outputs whose names happen to end in `.literate`.
Template discovery is per document: one invalid chapter must not hide another
chapter's dependencies.

``` ts : <<fragment repository member variables>>=+
private isWatchedInput(uri: vscode.Uri, folder: vscode.WorkspaceFolder): boolean {
  if (this.generatedOutputs.get(folder.uri.toString())?.has(uri.toString())) { return false; }
  const relative = path.posix.relative(folder.uri.path, uri.path);
  if (relative.split('/').slice(0, -1).some(part => discoveryExcludedDirectories.includes(part))) {
    return false;
  }
  if (relative.endsWith('.literate')) { return true; }
  const documents = new Map((this.sourceDocuments.get(folder.uri.toString()) || [])
    .map(document => [document.path, document]));
  for (const document of vscode.workspace.textDocuments) {
    if (!document.isClosed && document.uri.path.endsWith('.literate')
      && vscode.workspace.getWorkspaceFolder(document.uri)?.uri.toString() === folder.uri.toString()) {
      const name = path.posix.relative(folder.uri.path, document.uri.path);
      if (!name.split('/').slice(0, -1).some(part => discoveryExcludedDirectories.includes(part))) {
        documents.set(name, { path: name, text: document.getText() });
      }
    }
  }
  for (const document of documents.values()) {
    try {
      if (templateReferences([document]).includes(relative)) { return true; }
    } catch (_) {}
  }
  return false;
}
```

The *MarkdownIt* parser will handle the actual tokenizing and parsing of the
**literate** files.

Since we work with a multi-root workspace we'll create a map of maps. The keys
for this top-level map are full workspace URIs, not ambiguous display names. The actual
`FragmentMap`s will be the values to each workspace folder.

``` ts : <<fragment repository member variables>>=+
readonly fragmentsForWorkspaceFolders : Map<string, FragmentMap>;
```

For our parsing functionality we need an `Array<GrabbedState>`, which we have
encapsulated in the class `GrabbedStateList` and is available through the `list`
property. Each `GrabbedStateList` is keyed by the full workspace URI.

``` ts : <<fragment repository member variables>>=+
readonly grabbedStateForWorkspaceFolders : Map<string, GrabbedStateList>;
```

Finally we need a `DiagnosticCollection` to be able to keep track of detected
problems in **literate** projects. TBD: this probably needs to be changed into a
map of `DiagnosticCollection`, again with the workspace folder names as keys.

``` ts : <<fragment repository member variables>>=+
readonly diagnostics : vscode.DiagnosticCollection;
```

#### Constructor

The constructor takes an extension context to register any disposables there.
We'll be registering to text document changes, and to workspace changes. In both
cases we want to process **literate** files to regenerate fragments, source
files and HTML files.

``` ts : <<fragment repository constructor>>=
constructor(
  context : vscode.ExtensionContext
)
{
  <<initializing the fragment repository members>>

  <<subscribe to text document changes>>
  <<subscribe to workspace changes>>
  context.subscriptions.push(...this.inputSubscriptions);
}
```


##### Initializing members

First we make sure we have an instance of the *MarkdownIt* parser that is set up
for our **literate** files processing.


``` ts : <<initializing the fragment repository members>>=
this.md = createMarkdownItParserForLiterate();
```

Then we'll make sure the maps for tracking fragment maps and grabbed states are
created, and finally pushing our diagnostics collection to our subscription.

``` ts : <<initializing the fragment repository members>>=+
this.fragmentsForWorkspaceFolders = new Map<string, FragmentMap>();
this.grabbedStateForWorkspaceFolders = new Map<string, GrabbedStateList>();
this.diagnostics = vscode.languages.createDiagnosticCollection('literate');
context.subscriptions.push(this.diagnostics);
```

##### Subscribing to text document changes

The repository processes every literate content change, including an opening
chevron. Completion refreshes are read-only, and generated source/HTML changes
are ignored so they cannot create a generation feedback loop.

``` ts :  <<subscribe to text document changes>>=
this.inputSubscriptions.push(
  vscode.workspace.onDidChangeTextDocument(
    async (e : vscode.TextDocumentChangeEvent) =>
    {
      const folder = vscode.workspace.getWorkspaceFolder(e.document.uri);
      if (!this.disposed && e.contentChanges.length > 0 && folder
        && this.isWatchedInput(e.document.uri, folder)) {
        const key = folder.uri.toString();
        clearTimeout(this.inputTimers.get(key));
        this.inputTimers.delete(key);
        await this.processLiterateFiles(e.document, true).catch(() => undefined);
      }
    }
  )
);
```

##### Subscribing to workspace changes

Triggering of processing **literate** documents is necessary when new workspace
folders have been added. Additionally we need to clean up fragment maps and
grabbed states for those workspace folders that have been removed from the
workspace folder.

``` ts : <<subscribe to workspace changes>>=
const watcher = vscode.workspace.createFileSystemWatcher('**/*');
const changed = (uri: vscode.Uri) => {
  const folder = vscode.workspace.getWorkspaceFolder(uri);
  if (this.disposed || !folder || !this.isWatchedInput(uri, folder)) { return; }
  const key = folder.uri.toString();
  clearTimeout(this.inputTimers.get(key));
  this.inputTimers.set(key, setTimeout(() => {
    this.inputTimers.delete(key);
    if (!this.disposed) {
      void this.processLiterateFiles(folder, true).catch(() => undefined);
    }
  }, 100));
};
this.inputSubscriptions.push(watcher, watcher.onDidCreate(changed),
  watcher.onDidChange(changed), watcher.onDidDelete(changed));
```

Filesystem events above share a short per-workspace debounce, coalescing bursts
without delaying unrelated roots. Workspace membership changes have a different
lifetime: additions need an initial snapshot, while removals must discard both
pending timers and every cache keyed by that root's URI.

``` ts : <<subscribe to workspace changes>>=+
this.inputSubscriptions.push(
  vscode.workspace.onDidChangeWorkspaceFolders(
    async (e : vscode.WorkspaceFoldersChangeEvent) =>
    {
      for(const addedWorkspaceFolder of e.added) {
        await this.processLiterateFiles(addedWorkspaceFolder, true).catch(() => undefined);
      }
      for(const removedWorkspaceFolder of e.removed)
      {
        const key = removedWorkspaceFolder.uri.toString();
        clearTimeout(this.inputTimers.get(key));
        this.inputTimers.delete(key);
        this.generatedOutputs.delete(key);
        this.fragmentsForWorkspaceFolders.delete(key);
        this.grabbedStateForWorkspaceFolders.delete(key);
        this.sourceDocuments.delete(key);
        this.previewThemes.delete(key);
        this.occurrences.delete(key);
      }
    }
  )
);
```

#### Processing literate files

The extension is an I/O adapter for the same pure `generateProject` engine used
by the CLI. It snapshots every discovered document, preferring open editor
buffers (including empty unsaved buffers), and loads the templates requested by
`templateReferences`. Paths returned by that helper have already been checked
by the engine. Missing template files are omitted so the engine can report the
error at the declaration, rather than hiding it behind a filesystem exception.
Other I/O failures reject the operation and are reported to the user. If template
discovery rejects an invalid declaration, generation still runs to diagnose it
at its source location. If document ordering rejects invalid input, the engine
has already diagnosed it and the navigation parser does not parse it again.

`processLiterateFiles` defaults to a read-only refresh for completion providers.
Activation, content changes and the Process command explicitly request writes.
All requests share a serial queue: parsing and writing cannot overlap, and a
failed request does not poison later requests. The returned promise covers the
whole operation, including directory creation and file writes. Generated source
and HTML changes do not themselves trigger processing.

The engine owns validation, expansion, settings, templates and HTML generation.
Its zero-based diagnostic positions map directly to VS Code ranges. Diagnostics
are replaced only for the workspace being processed, preserving other folders.
An invalid project produces no writes; existing outputs remain untouched. For a
valid project the adapter compares UTF-8 bytes with disk, creates parent
directories, and awaits writes only for changed files. This avoids unnecessary
watcher events and preserves the engine's identical output across platforms.
Changed outputs are staged as a batch, installed by rename, and rolled back on
caught failures. Originals stay available until commit; failed recovery reports
retained backup locations. This is not crash-durable or simultaneously visible
across all files. Obsolete outputs are not deleted.

The navigation map is deliberately unexpanded and is built from the same
snapshot, ordered by the engine's `orderSourceDocuments`: root index first,
linked chapters next, and every remaining document in deterministic order.
This map is not another generator and never supplies generation diagnostics.

A content-change request follows this sequence. Navigation providers use the
same refresh path but omit the output-writing branch. The serial queue keeps a
second request waiting until the first has finished, including its writes.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant VS as VS Code
    participant Repo as Fragment repository
    participant FS as Workspace filesystem
    participant Engine as Shared engine
    VS->>Repo: Process literate change
    Note over Repo: Wait for earlier queued request
    Repo->>FS: Discover documents and read saved inputs
    Note over Repo: Prefer open buffers for documents and templates
    Repo->>Engine: Generate from the snapshot
    Engine-->>Repo: Diagnostics, inspection and planned files
    Repo-->>VS: Update diagnostics and navigation state
    alt Writes requested and no diagnostics
        Repo->>FS: Preflight every output path
        alt All destinations are safe
            Repo->>FS: Compare all disk bytes and stage changed files
            loop Each changed output
                Repo->>FS: Back up original and install stage by rename
            end
            alt Installation succeeds
                Repo->>FS: Remove private staging material
            else Staging or installation fails
                Repo->>FS: Restore originals and remove new outputs
                Note over Repo,FS: Keep recovery material if rollback fails
            end
        else Unsafe destination or filesystem error
            Repo-->>VS: Report failure without starting output writes
        end
    else Read-only refresh or invalid project
        Note over Repo,FS: No output writes
    end
    Repo-->>VS: Complete request
```

``` ts : <<fragment generation method>>=
getPreviewProject(document: vscode.Uri | undefined, source: string | undefined) {
  const folder = document && vscode.workspace.getWorkspaceFolder(document);
  const currentPath = folder && document ? path.posix.relative(folder.uri.path, document.path) : 'index.literate';
  const documents = new Map((folder ? this.sourceDocuments.get(folder.uri.toString()) || [] : [])
    .map(value => [value.path, value]));
  for (const open of vscode.workspace.textDocuments) {
    if (folder && !open.isClosed && open.uri.path.endsWith('.literate')
      && vscode.workspace.getWorkspaceFolder(open.uri)?.uri.toString() === folder.uri.toString()) {
      const name = path.posix.relative(folder.uri.path, open.uri.path);
      if (safeRelativePath(name) === name && open.uri.toString() === vscode.Uri.joinPath(folder.uri, name).toString()
        && !name.split('/').slice(0, -1).some(value => discoveryExcludedDirectories.includes(value))) {
        documents.set(name, { path: name, text: open.getText() });
      }
    }
  }
  if (source !== undefined) { documents.set(currentPath, { path: currentPath, text: source }); }
  const inputs = [...documents.values()];
  const parsed = parseProject(inputs);
  return { path: currentPath, publication: parsed.publication, annotations: parsed.annotations,
      paths: parsed.documents.map(value => value.path),
    tokens: parsed.documents.find(value => value.path === currentPath)?.tokens,
    theme: folder ? previewThemeName(inputs) : defaultThemeName };
}
```

Preview rendering is synchronous and read-only. Cached host tokens retain their
original source in a weak map; rendering overlays that exact source and all other
open buffers on the last discovered project snapshot. Capture source before
Markdown-it normalizes NUL characters: invalid source must not acquire semantic
targets merely by being displayed. The same overlay selects the root theme.
Missing families stay unlinked. Filesystem discovery and save-time output
transactions remain separate.

```ts : <<fragment generation method>>=+
getPreviewTheme(document?: vscode.Uri): string {
  const folder = document && vscode.workspace.getWorkspaceFolder(document);
  if (!folder) { return defaultThemeName; }
  const index = vscode.Uri.joinPath(folder.uri, 'index.literate').toString();
  const openIndex = vscode.workspace.textDocuments.find(doc => !doc.isClosed && doc.uri.toString() === index);
  return openIndex
    ? previewThemeName([{ path: 'index.literate', text: openIndex.getText() }])
    : this.previewThemes.get(folder.uri.toString()) ?? defaultThemeName;
}
```

Processing refreshes the cached theme alongside the document snapshot. A changed
selection requests a preview refresh without waiting on rendering, so the serial
processing queue cannot deadlock with a preview request.

``` ts : <<fragment generation method>>=+
processLiterateFiles(
  trigger : vscode.WorkspaceFolder | vscode.TextDocument | undefined,
  writeFiles = false
) : Promise<void> {
  const operation = this.processingQueue.then(async () => {
    if (this.disposed) { return; }
    const folder = trigger && ("eol" in trigger
      ? determineWorkspaceFolder(trigger) : trigger);
    const folders = trigger ? (folder ? [folder] : [])
      : vscode.workspace.workspaceFolders || [];
    for (const workspaceFolder of folders) {
      <<snapshot workspace documents and theme>>
      <<load snapshot templates>>
      const result = generateProject(documents, templates);
      const previousPreview = parseProject(this.sourceDocuments.get(themeKey) || []);
            const previousPublication = previousPreview.publication;
      <<translate generation diagnostics>>
      <<replace workspace diagnostics>>
      <<prepare ordered navigation snapshot>>
      <<parse navigation snapshot>>
      <<refresh semantic preview snapshot>>
      <<commit valid requested outputs>>
    }
  });
  this.processingQueue = operation.catch(error => {
    void vscode.window.showErrorMessage(`Literate processing failed: ${String(error)}`);
  });
  return operation;
}
```

Publication and generation share one serial queue. An export owns the queue from
source capture through helper cleanup, preventing editor-driven generation from
changing inputs or outputs while a book is being published. Failure advances the
queue without replacing the error reported by the publication command.

```ts : <<fragment generation method>>=+
withPublicationOperation<T>(operation: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {
  const pending = this.processingQueue.then(() => {
    if (this.disposed) { throw new Error('Literate repository is disposed'); }
    return operation();
  });
  this.processingQueue = pending.then(() => {}, () => {});
  return pending;
}
```

A semantic refresh is requested only after installing the new navigation/source
snapshot, so a host that starts rendering immediately cannot see old destinations.
It is not awaited and does not change whether output writes are requested.

```ts : <<refresh semantic preview snapshot>>=
if (theme !== previousTheme || JSON.stringify(previousPublication.contributions) !== JSON.stringify(result.publication.contributions)
  || JSON.stringify(previousPublication.fragments) !== JSON.stringify(result.publication.fragments)
    || JSON.stringify(previousPreview.annotations) !== JSON.stringify(parseProject(documents).annotations)) {
  void vscode.commands.executeCommand('markdown.preview.refresh').then(undefined, () => undefined);
}
```

##### Snapshot inputs before generation

Every downstream view uses the same document contents. Theme selection happens
before template loading and generation; refresh follows snapshot installation,
not output writes. The refresh is deliberately not awaited by the queue.

``` ts : <<snapshot workspace documents and theme>>=
const documents: SourceDocument[] = [];
for (const uri of await getLiterateFileUris(workspaceFolder)) {
  documents.push({
    path: path.posix.relative(workspaceFolder.uri.path, uri.path),
    text: await getFileContent(uri)
  });
}
const themeKey = workspaceFolder.uri.toString();
const theme = previewThemeName(documents);
const previousTheme = this.previewThemes.get(themeKey) ?? defaultThemeName;
this.previewThemes.set(themeKey, theme);
```

Template discovery can reject malformed declarations, but that must not suppress
the engine's source diagnostics. Likewise a missing template is represented by
its absence in `templates`. Other read failures abort this queued operation
rather than supplying a misleading partial snapshot.

``` ts : <<load snapshot templates>>=
const templates: Record<string, string> = Object.create(null);
let references: string[] = [];
try {
  references = templateReferences(documents);
} catch (_) {}
for (const reference of references) {
  try {
    templates[reference] = await getFileContent(
      vscode.Uri.joinPath(workspaceFolder.uri, reference));
  } catch (error) {
    if (!(error instanceof vscode.FileSystemError) || error.code !== 'FileNotFound') {
      throw error;
    }
  }
}
```

##### Publish diagnostics for one workspace

The engine's relative paths become full document URIs at the editor boundary.
Grouping first lets the adapter replace a document's entries together, retaining
engine diagnostic codes for consumers of the Problems collection.

``` ts : <<translate generation diagnostics>>=
const diagnostics = new Map<string, vscode.Diagnostic[]>();
for (const diagnostic of result.diagnostics) {
  const uri = vscode.Uri.joinPath(workspaceFolder.uri, diagnostic.path);
  const key = uri.toString();
  const entries = diagnostics.get(key) || [];
  const range = new vscode.Range(diagnostic.line, diagnostic.column,
    diagnostic.line, diagnostic.column + 1);
  const entry = new vscode.Diagnostic(range, diagnostic.message,
    vscode.DiagnosticSeverity.Error);
  if (diagnostic.code !== undefined) { entry.code = diagnostic.code; }
  entries.push(entry);
  diagnostics.set(key, entries);
}
```

A successful correction can leave no new entries at all. Removing the old set
before publishing the new one clears those resolved errors without disturbing
diagnostics owned by other workspace folders.

``` ts : <<replace workspace diagnostics>>=
const stale: vscode.Uri[] = [];
this.diagnostics.forEach(uri => {
  if (vscode.workspace.getWorkspaceFolder(uri)?.uri.toString() === workspaceFolder.uri.toString()) {
    stale.push(uri);
  }
});
for (const uri of stale) { this.diagnostics.delete(uri); }
for (const [uri, entries] of diagnostics) {
  this.diagnostics.set(vscode.Uri.parse(uri), entries);
}
```

##### Rebuild navigation before considering writes

Navigation must not retain an earlier valid snapshot when ordering the new one
fails. Install empty containers first, then publish the ordered source and its
occurrences. These fragments expand inside the workspace loop: `continue`
skips that workspace's remaining work, not the whole queued request.

``` ts : <<prepare ordered navigation snapshot>>=
const states = new GrabbedStateList();
const fragments = new FragmentMap();
const key = workspaceFolder.uri.toString();
this.grabbedStateForWorkspaceFolders.set(key, states);
this.fragmentsForWorkspaceFolders.set(key, fragments);
this.sourceDocuments.set(key, []);
this.occurrences.set(key, []);
let ordered: SourceDocument[];
try {
  ordered = orderSourceDocuments(documents);
          this.occurrences.set(key, collectFragmentOccurrences(ordered));
          this.sourceDocuments.set(key, ordered);
} catch (_) {
  continue;
}
```

The parser adds editor token locations to the ordered snapshot. Accepted fragment
parts still come from engine inspection, so navigation does not introduce a
second declaration-acceptance policy or expand code of its own.

``` ts : <<parse navigation snapshot>>=
for (const document of ordered) {
  const env: GrabbedState = {
    literateFileName: document.path,
    literateUri: vscode.Uri.joinPath(workspaceFolder.uri, document.path),
    gstate: new StateCore('', this.md, {})
  };
  this.md.parse(document.text, env);
  states.list.push(env);
}
fragments.map = handleFragments(states.list, result.inspection.fragments);
```

Only the final step depends on write intent and a diagnostic-free result.
Remembering destinations before filesystem work prevents output-shaped input
events from feeding back into generation. Preflight covers the whole batch
before the transaction coordinator is allowed to stage changes.

``` ts : <<commit valid requested outputs>>=
if (!writeFiles || result.diagnostics.length) { continue; }
this.generatedOutputs.set(key, new Set(result.files.map(file =>
  vscode.Uri.joinPath(workspaceFolder.uri, file.path).toString())));
const outputs = await this.preflightOutputs(workspaceFolder.uri, result.files);
await this.writeChangedOutputs(outputs);
```

#### Preflighting output paths

Before reading any output contents, preflight every source and HTML output in
this project. The shared `safeRelativePath` policy keeps paths beneath the trusted
workspace root; only components below that boundary are inspected. Filesystem
access stays in `workspace.fs`, including for remote workspace URIs.

Check the symbolic-link bit before checking the exact expected type: providers
can combine `SymbolicLink` with `File` or `Directory`. Unknown and special types
are not safe substitutes. A missing component ends the walk, since descendants
cannot exist beneath it. However, `stat` may follow a dangling link and report
`FileNotFound`. In that case inspect the already-checked parent's directory
entries before treating the component as absent. Other errors must propagate.

``` ts : <<fragment generation method>>=+
private async preflightOutputs(
  root: vscode.Uri, files: readonly { path: string; content: string }[]
): Promise<{ files: TransactionFile[]; io: TransactionIO }> {
  const io = this.outputTransactionIO(root);
  const outputs: TransactionFile[] = [];
  for (const file of files) {
    if (await io.inspect(file.path) === 'directory') {
      throw new Error(`Unsafe output path (expected file): ${file.path}`);
    }
    outputs.push({ path: file.path, content: Buffer.from(file.content, 'utf-8') });
  }
  return { files: outputs, io };
}
```

The filesystem adapter supplies the transaction's small I/O contract. It keeps
URIs intact for remote providers and validates every relative path before use.
Generated destinations are registered before staging or committing can emit
input-shaped watcher events.

``` ts : <<fragment generation method>>=+
private outputTransactionIO(root: vscode.Uri): TransactionIO {
  const modes = new Map<string, number>();
  <<resolve transaction URI>>
  <<inspect transaction path>>
  <<require transaction entry type>>
  <<require transaction parent>>
  <<remove empty transaction directory>>
  return {
    inspect,
    <<read transaction file>>
    <<create transaction directory>>
    <<write new transaction file>>
    <<rename transaction file>>
    <<remove transaction file>>
    removeDirectory,
    nonce: randomUUID
  };
}
```

The adapter's helpers close over one trusted root and one permission snapshot.
URI construction validates the relative spelling even for operations that have
already inspected the path; no provider operation should bypass that boundary.

``` ts : <<resolve transaction URI>>=
const uriFor = (relative: string): vscode.Uri => {
  const safe = safeRelativePath(relative);
  if (!safe) { throw new Error(`Unsafe output path: ${relative}`); }
  return vscode.Uri.joinPath(root, safe);
};
```

Inspection walks from the trusted root towards the leaf. An absent component
ends the walk, whereas links or unsuitable intermediate types reject it. A leaf
may be either a file or a directory; each operation imposes its own narrower
requirement afterward.

``` ts : <<inspect transaction path>>=
const inspect: TransactionIO['inspect'] = async relativePath => {
  const relative = safeRelativePath(relativePath);
  if (!relative) { throw new Error(`Unsafe output path: ${relativePath}`); }
  const parts = relative.split('/');
  let parent = root;
  for (let index = 0; index < parts.length; index++) {
    const uri = vscode.Uri.joinPath(parent, parts[index]);
    const type = await this.outputEntryType(parent, uri, parts[index]);
    if (type === undefined) { return 'missing'; }
    if (type & vscode.FileType.SymbolicLink) {
      throw new Error(`Unsafe output path (symbolic link): ${uri.toString()}`);
    }
    const leaf = index === parts.length - 1;
    if (type !== vscode.FileType.Directory && !(leaf && type === vscode.FileType.File)) {
      throw new Error(`Unsafe output path (expected ${leaf ? 'file or directory' : 'directory'}): ${uri.toString()}`);
    }
    if (leaf) { return type === vscode.FileType.File ? 'file' : 'directory'; }
    parent = uri;
  }
  throw new Error(`Unsafe output path: ${relativePath}`);
};
```

Preflight alone is not enough for a multi-step transaction: staging and rollback
change which entries should exist. Each operation rechecks the expected state
at its own boundary, using the same inspection policy.

``` ts : <<require transaction entry type>>=
const requireType = async (relative: string, expected: 'missing' | 'file' | 'directory'): Promise<void> => {
  if (await inspect(relative) !== expected) {
    throw new Error(`Unsafe output path (expected ${expected}): ${relative}`);
  }
};
```

Creation requires an existing immediate parent. For a direct child of the trusted
root there is no relative parent to inspect, so a directory listing checks that
the provider can access the root instead.

``` ts : <<require transaction parent>>=
const requireParent = async (relative: string): Promise<void> => {
  const safe = safeRelativePath(relative);
  if (!safe) { throw new Error(`Unsafe output path: ${relative}`); }
  const parent = safe.split('/').slice(0, -1).join('/');
  if (parent) { await requireType(parent, 'directory'); }
  else { await vscode.workspace.fs.readDirectory(root); }
};
```

Cleanup is intentionally nonrecursive. A directory containing unexpected entries
must survive for recovery rather than allowing rollback to erase unrelated data.

``` ts : <<remove empty transaction directory>>=
const removeDirectory = async (relative: string): Promise<void> => {
  await requireType(relative, 'directory');
  const uri = uriFor(relative);
  if ((await vscode.workspace.fs.readDirectory(uri)).length) {
    throw new Error(`Output directory is not empty: ${relative}`);
  }
  await vscode.workspace.fs.delete(uri, { recursive: false, useTrash: false });
};
```

Reading a local original also records its permission bits for later replacement.
The extra local `lstat` rejects links and non-files; remote providers stay on the
portable workspace filesystem API and do not contribute mode metadata.

``` ts : <<read transaction file>>=
read: async relative => {
  await requireType(relative, 'file');
  const uri = uriFor(relative);
  if (uri.scheme === 'file') {
    const stat = await lstat(uri.fsPath);
    if (stat.isSymbolicLink() || !stat.isFile()) {
      throw new Error(`Unsafe output path (expected file): ${uri.toString()}`);
    }
    modes.set(uri.toString(), stat.mode & 0o7777);
  }
  return vscode.workspace.fs.readFile(uri);
},
```

A provider may throw after creating a directory. On that failure path we attempt
to remove only a confirmed empty directory. If its removal also fails, the
special transaction error lets the coordinator take responsibility for recovery;
otherwise the original creation error remains the failure reported.

``` ts : <<create transaction directory>>=
mkdir: async relative => {
  await requireType(relative, 'missing');
  await requireParent(relative);
  try { await vscode.workspace.fs.createDirectory(uriFor(relative)); }
  catch (error) {
    let createdEmptyDirectory = false;
    try {
      if (await inspect(relative) === 'directory') {
        if ((await vscode.workspace.fs.readDirectory(uriFor(relative))).length) {
          throw new Error(`Output directory is not empty: ${relative}`);
        }
        createdEmptyDirectory = true;
        await removeDirectory(relative);
      }
    } catch (cleanupError) {
      const message = `Directory creation failed: ${relative}; ${String(error)}; empty-directory cleanup failed: ${String(cleanupError)}`;
      if (createdEmptyDirectory) { throw new TransactionDirectoryError(relative, message); }
      throw new Error(message);
    }
    throw error;
  }
},
```

Stage writes require absence and an accessible parent. These checks express the
transaction's no-overwrite intent, though the provider API does not make the
check and write an exclusive filesystem operation.

``` ts : <<write new transaction file>>=
writeNew: async (relative, content) => {
  await requireType(relative, 'missing');
  await requireParent(relative);
  await vscode.workspace.fs.writeFile(uriFor(relative), content);
},
```

Renames serve both installation and rollback. Permission restoration applies
only when installing a local stage over a previously read destination; backup
restoration moves the original itself. Non-overwriting rename protects an
unexpected destination rather than silently replacing it.

``` ts : <<rename transaction file>>=
rename: async (from, to) => {
  await requireType(from, 'file');
  await requireType(to, 'missing');
  await requireParent(to);
  const source = uriFor(from), target = uriFor(to);
  const mode = modes.get(target.toString());
  if (source.scheme === 'file' && from.endsWith('.stage') && mode !== undefined) {
    await chmod(source.fsPath, mode);
  }
  await vscode.workspace.fs.rename(source, target, { overwrite: false });
},
```

File cleanup shares the same type checks and never requests recursive deletion.
Directory cleanup is supplied separately, keeping the transaction's two kinds
of recovery action distinct.

``` ts : <<remove transaction file>>=
removeFile: async relative => {
  await requireType(relative, 'file');
  await vscode.workspace.fs.delete(uriFor(relative), { recursive: false, useTrash: false });
},
```

The entry lookup distinguishes an absent path from a dangling link. Only a
`FileNotFound` response permits the directory-entry fallback; permission and
provider failures must stop generation rather than authorize a write.

``` ts : <<fragment generation method>>=+
private async outputEntryType(parent: vscode.Uri, uri: vscode.Uri, name: string): Promise<vscode.FileType | undefined> {
  try {
    return (await vscode.workspace.fs.stat(uri)).type;
  } catch (error) {
    if (!(error instanceof vscode.FileSystemError) || error.code !== 'FileNotFound') {
      throw error;
    }
  }
  const entries = await vscode.workspace.fs.readDirectory(parent);
  return entries.find(([entry]) => entry === name)?.[1];
}
```

These checks depend on the provider reporting links through `stat` or directory
entries. VS Code has no portable no-follow open/write API: concurrent filesystem
changes can race the checks (TOCTOU). VS Code also has no exclusive mkdir or
exclusive writeFile operation. The adapter checks absence and the immediate
parent before creation, assuming no concurrent external changes. If directory
creation throws after taking effect, it attempts to remove the empty directory.
If a confirmed empty created directory cannot be removed, `TransactionDirectoryError`
transfers ownership to the coordinator for rollback cleanup; persistent failure
reports that artifact's recovery path along with the creation and cleanup errors.
The workspace URI is the trusted boundary (not statted); directory listing checks
that boundary when checking a missing immediate child or creating beneath it.
Providers must honor non-overwriting move semantics and nonrecursive deletion.
This is recoverable exception handling, not a security sandbox, crash-durable
journal, or simultaneous atomic visibility across outputs. Rollback failures
preserve recovery locations and propagate the transaction error.
Read-only navigation refreshes bypass both output preflight and output writes.

#### Writing changed outputs

Only after the entire project's preflight succeeds does the shared coordinator
compare disk bytes, stage every changed file, and install the batch by rename.
Open output buffers are not used for this comparison. Each workspace remains a
separate transaction. Numbered stage and backup names do not end in `.literate`,
so their watcher events do not trigger input processing.

For local `file` URIs, reading an existing output snapshots its POSIX permission
bits with Node `lstat`; `chmod` applies those bits to the stage before installation.
Rollback moves the original backup back without changing its mode. New outputs
use provider defaults. Remote URIs use only `workspace.fs`, which exposes no mode
API: replacement metadata follows provider defaults, and remote permission or
metadata restoration is not guaranteed. Ownership, ACLs and other metadata are
not preserved by this local permission-bit handling either.

``` ts : <<fragment generation method>>=+
private async writeChangedOutputs(outputs: { files: readonly TransactionFile[]; io: TransactionIO }): Promise<void> {
  await commitOutputs(outputs.files, outputs.io);
}
```

#### Fetching fragments for workspace folder

When we call `getFragments` we assume the **literate** projects have all been
process properly. In most cases that is triggered automatically, but it may be
necessary to trigger the processing manually before calling `getFragments`. When
the projects have been properly processed, though, this function returns the
`FragmentMap` for the given workspace folder.

``` ts : <<method to get fragments from repository>>=
getFragments(workspaceFolder : vscode.WorkspaceFolder) : FragmentMap
{
  return this.fragmentsForWorkspaceFolders.get(workspaceFolder.uri.toString()) || new FragmentMap();
}
```

#### Getting fragment on line for position

This method checks to see if for the given text line and position a fragment
usage or mention can be found.

The shared symbol collector supplies exact positions in the original document.
Lookup uses the repository instance and the full workspace URI, so repeated tags
and equally named workspace folders remain independent.

``` ts : <<method to get fragment on line for position>>=
getFragmentTagLocation(
  document: vscode.TextDocument,
  currentLine: vscode.TextLine,
  position: vscode.Position
): FragmentLocation {
  const folder = determineWorkspaceFolder(document);
  if (!folder) { return unsetFragmentLocation; }
  const documentPath = path.posix.relative(folder.uri.path, document.uri.path);
  const occurrence = this.getFragmentOccurrences(folder).find(item =>
    item.path === documentPath && item.line === currentLine.lineNumber &&
    item.column <= position.character && position.character < item.endColumn);
  if (!occurrence) { return unsetFragmentLocation; }
  const range = new vscode.Range(occurrence.line, occurrence.column,
    occurrence.line, occurrence.endColumn);
  return new FragmentLocation(occurrence.name, document.uri, range,
    this.getFragments(folder).map.get(occurrence.name),
    occurrence.kind === 'definition' || occurrence.kind === 'append' ? '=' : undefined,
    occurrence.kind === 'append' ? '+' : undefined);
}
```

##### The FragmentLocation

A fragment location encodes the occurrence of what could be a fragment that
already exists or one that still needs to be defined. The class holds the name
of the fragment, the range of this string in the resource specified by the uri,
and whether a `FragmentInformation` was found or not.

The properties `root` and `add` can be used to determine what type of fragment
is at the given range.

``` ts : <<fragment tag location>>=
export class FragmentLocation
{
  readonly rangeExclusive : vscode.Range;
  readonly valid : boolean;

  constructor(
    public readonly name : string,
    public readonly uri: vscode.Uri,
    public readonly range : vscode.Range,
    public readonly fragment : FragmentInformation | undefined,
    public readonly root : string | undefined,
    public readonly add : string | undefined
  )
  {
    this.valid = uri.fsPath.indexOf('not_valid_for_literate')===-1;
    if(this.valid && name.length > 0) {
      this.rangeExclusive = new vscode.Range(
        range.start.line, range.start.character + 2,
        range.end.line, range.end.character - 2
      );
    }
    else
    {
      this.rangeExclusive = range;
    }
  }
}
```

Failed lookups share a sentinel location rather than returning `undefined`. Its
special URI keeps `valid` false, so the empty range is not treated as a tag whose
delimiters can be stripped.

``` ts : <<fragment tag location>>=+
const unsetFragmentLocation =
    new FragmentLocation(
      '',
      vscode.Uri.file('not_valid_for_literate'),
      new vscode.Range(0,0,0,0),
      undefined,
      undefined,
      undefined
    );
```

#### Get fragment usage token

This method takes a text document and a range, based on which the token
containing the range is returned. If no token is found, or the workspace folder
is not available the `emptyToken` constant is returned.

```ts : <<method to get token at position>>=
getTokenAtPosition(
  document : vscode.TextDocument,
  range : vscode.Range
) : TokenUsage
{
```

Determine the workspace folder for the given text document. As mentioned above,
is no workspace folder is found the `emptyToken` is returned.

```ts : <<method to get token at position>>=+
  const workspaceFolder : vscode.WorkspaceFolder | undefined = determineWorkspaceFolder(document);
  if(!workspaceFolder)
  {
    return emptyToken;
  }
```

Next we can retrieve the state for the document.

```ts : <<method to get token at position>>=+
  const state = this.getDocumentState(document);
```

We can iterate over all the tokens in the grabbed state of the document. We're
only interested in tokens that have a valid `map` property, since we need to
check the range asked for.

```    ts    : <<method to get token at position>>=+
  for(const token of state.gstate.tokens)
  {
    if(token.map) {
```

If the range given is contained within the token map we create a new TokenUsage
and return that. This concludes the search for the token containing the range we
are interested in.

```     ts     : <<method to get token at position>>=+
      const tokenRange = new vscode.Range(token.map[0], 0, token.map[1], 1024);
      if(tokenRange.contains(range))
      {
        let tokenUsage : TokenUsage = {
          token : token,
        };
        return tokenUsage;
      }
    }
  }
```

If no hit was found return the `emptyToken`.

```ts : <<method to get token at position>>=+
  return emptyToken;
}
```

The `TokenUsage` interface helps determining whether we have a token or not.
TBD: we can probably get rid of this interface and just use a `Token` directly.

``` ts : <<token usage interface>>=
interface TokenUsage
{
  token : Token | undefined,
}

const emptyToken : TokenUsage =
{
  token : undefined,
};
```

#### Get list of grabbed states for a workspace

``` ts : <<method to get state for workspace>>=
getWorkspaceState(workspaceFolder : vscode.WorkspaceFolder) : GrabbedStateList
{
  return this.grabbedStateForWorkspaceFolders.get(workspaceFolder.uri.toString()) || new GrabbedStateList();
}
```

#### Get the grabbed state of a document

``` ts : <<method to get state for document>>=
getDocumentState(document: vscode.TextDocument) : GrabbedState
{
  let grabbedState : GrabbedState = emptyState;
  const ws = determineWorkspaceFolder(document);
  if(ws) {
    const workspaceState = this.getWorkspaceState(ws);
    for(const state of workspaceState.list)
    {
      if(document.uri.toString() === state.literateUri.toString())
      {
        grabbedState = state;
      }
    }
  }

  return grabbedState;
}
```

#### Get all reference locations

References and explicit prose mentions come from the same snapshot used for
navigation and rename. Declaration inclusion is a caller choice and includes
append headers as well as the base definition.

``` ts : <<method to get all reference locations>>=
getReferenceLocations(
  workspaceFolder: vscode.WorkspaceFolder,
  fragmentName: string,
  includeDeclaration = false
): vscode.Location[] {
  return this.getFragmentOccurrences(workspaceFolder)
    .filter(item => item.name === fragmentName &&
      (includeDeclaration || item.kind === 'reference' || item.kind === 'mention'))
    .map(item => new vscode.Location(vscode.Uri.joinPath(workspaceFolder.uri, item.path),
      new vscode.Range(item.line, item.column, item.line, item.endColumn)));
}
```

The rename planner consumes original source text rather than rendered tokens.
These read-only snapshots include unsaved buffers, refreshed before a provider
answers an editor request.

``` ts : <<method to get all reference locations>>=+
getSourceDocuments(folder: vscode.WorkspaceFolder): SourceDocument[] {
  return this.sourceDocuments.get(folder.uri.toString()) || [];
}
```

Occurrence ranges retain UTF-16 columns, matching VS Code's position model.

``` ts : <<method to get all reference locations>>=+
getFragmentOccurrences(folder: vscode.WorkspaceFolder): FragmentOccurrence[] {
  return this.occurrences.get(folder.uri.toString()) || [];
}
```

## Collecting editor state

The repository parses the engine-ordered document snapshot with the grabber
plugin to retain Markdown token locations for navigation. Parsing here does
not render or write HTML; all generated files come from the engine.

### GrabbedState interface

The `GrabbedState` interface is used to create a type that helps us collecting
the tokens for each `.literate` file. Instances of objects with this interface
are passed to a *MarkdownIt* renderer. The renderer will have the
`GrabberPlugin` registered, which provides a rule that helps us collecting the
states of each rendered file. The grabbed state is collected in `gstate`, which
is an instance of the `StateCore`, provided by *MarkdownIt*.

The interface defines `literateFileName`, which is the filename of the
**literate** document to which the grabbed state belongs. `literateUri` is the
full uri for this document. Finally `gstate` holds the `StateCore` of the
parsing result.

``` ts : <<grabbed state type>>=
interface GrabbedState {
  literateFileName: string;
  literateUri: vscode.Uri;
  gstate: StateCore;
}
```

We define a `GrabbedState` that is not valid, the `emptyState`. This allows us
to always return an object instead of `undefined` in select cases.

```  ts  : <<grabbed state type>>=+
const emptyState : GrabbedState =
{
  literateFileName : '',
  literateUri : vscode.Uri.file('not_valid_for_literate'),
  gstate: new StateCore('', createMarkdownItParserForLiterate(), '')
};
```

## Preparing MarkdownIt

In the `iterateLiterateFiles` we start by setting up the *MarkdownIt* parser.

``` ts : <<set up MarkdownIt>>=
const md : MarkdownIt = createMarkdownItParserForLiterate();
```

The function `createMarkdownItParserForLiterate` does this setup so that it is
easy to get a new parser to use for different purposes, like parsing documents
to get the code fragment names for code completion.

The highlight function we use to ensure our code fragments get syntax
highlighting. This simply relies on *highlight.js* to do the work.

We also tell *MarkdownIt* to use our `grabberPlugin`. This plug-in harvests the
internal states for each document into instances of `GrabbedState`. These states
we'll later use to get all the different code fragments and to weave them into
the code files they describe.

Finally `installPreviewFences` replaces the default `fence` rule with a
per-parser closure, as explained in the custom code fence rendering section.

``` ts : <<create markdownit parser>>=
function createMarkdownItParserForLiterate() : MarkdownIt
{
  const md : MarkdownIt = new MarkdownIt({
          highlight: function(str: string, lang: string, attrs: string) {
            if(lang && hljs.getLanguage(lang)) {
              return '<pre><code>' +
              hljs.highlight(str, {language : lang}).value +
              '</code></pre>';
            }
            return '<pre title="' + md.utils.escapeHtml(attrs) + '">' + md.utils.escapeHtml(str) + '</pre>';
          }

        })
        .use(grabberPlugin);

  installPreviewFences(md);
  return md;
}
```

## Fragment structure and regular expressions

Before we dive deeper into the processing of `.literate` documents it is
necessary to have a look at how fragments work.

Fragments in the `literate` extension have a specific format that requires a bit
of explaining.

There are four types of fragment tags, three of which either create or modify a
fragment, and one that expresses fragment usage.

Generation, preview and editor commands use the shared engine header grammar
and tag scanner. Only accepted declarations receive fragment decoration.

## Gathering editor fragments

The engine inspection identifies accepted definitions in deterministic order.
We attach the corresponding Markdown tokens for existing editor commands without
parsing declarations a second time. Unrelated generation errors need not prevent
navigation or a valid rename.

```ts : <<handle fragments>>=
function handleFragments(
  envList: GrabbedState[], inspected: FragmentInspection[]
): Map<string, FragmentInformation> {
  const fragments = new Map<string, FragmentInformation>();
  for (const entry of inspected) {
    const parts = entry.definitions.flatMap(definition => {
      const env = envList.find(item => item.literateFileName === definition.path);
      const token = env?.gstate.tokens.find(item =>
        item.type === 'fence' && item.map?.[0] === definition.line);
      return env && token ? [{ env, token }] : [];
    });
    if (!parts.length) { continue; }
    const env = parts[0].env;
    fragments.set(entry.name, {
      lang: entry.language, literateFileName: env.literateFileName,
      sourceFileName: entry.output || '', templateFileName: undefined,
      code: parts.map(part => part.token.content).join(''),
      tokens: parts.map(part => part.token), env
    });
  }
  return fragments;
}
```

### The FragmentInformation type

We have now seen the `FragmentInformation` type being used several times, so it
is important to take a moment to clarify it in more detail.

The interface allows us to gather information for each found code fragment. It
allows us to store the programming language identifier, name of the `.literate`
file and name of the targeted source file, if the code fragment happens to be a
top fragment.

The actual code for the fragment is stored in `code`. Furthermore the tokens for
the complete fragment are stored in the `tokens` list. This list is of objects
that fullfill the `Token` interface, which is provided by the *MarkdownIt*
module.

``` ts : <<fragment information type>>=
/**
 * Interface denoting a fragment and related information
 */
interface FragmentInformation {
  /**
   * Programming language identifier for fragment.
   */
  lang: string;
  /**
   * Filename of literate file.
   */
  literateFileName: string;
  /**
   * Filename of target source file. This is set when the fragment
   * is a top fragment.
   */
  sourceFileName: string;
```

The remaining fields retain the unexpanded representation needed by editor
features. Tokens can span several accepted parts; `env` belongs to the first
part, not necessarily to every token. The template slot remains optional (the
current editor collector leaves it unset); generation resolves templates in the
engine rather than through this navigation record.

``` ts : <<fragment information type>>=+
  templateFileName: vscode.Uri | undefined;
  code: string;
  tokens: Token[];
  env: GrabbedState;
}
```

### One output generator

Expansion, source templates and rendered book files are implemented only in the
engine. The extension's legacy extrapolator and source/HTML writers have been
removed; preview rendering below is exclusively for VS Code's live Markdown
preview, not the generated book.

## custom code fence rendering

Our extension uses a custom code fence rendering rule for the live Markdown
preview to ensure the code fragment name is also rendered as part of the fence.
The generated book uses the engine renderer instead.

Each installation captures the parser's existing fence rule, never a shared
mutable renderer. Configuration fences stay hidden. Plain fences whose trimmed
info is exactly `mermaid` bypass highlighting and expose escaped, original source
to the bundled browser client through `pre.literate-mermaid`. The client reads
`textContent` and owns rendering and error handling; source-line metadata keeps
VS Code preview navigation working.

Only the preview parser wraps `renderer.render`, not `parse` or `renderInline`.
VS Code supplies `env.currentDocument` as a URI at render time, even when it
reuses cached tokens (see its `markdown-language-features/src/markdownEngine.ts`).
The wrapper selects the owning workspace's root index theme, preferring an open
buffer over the repository snapshot. Local chapter settings and standalone
Markdown settings never override the project theme. Invalid theme settings use
the shared default; diagnostics remain the generation engine's responsibility.
The browser client supplies scoped CSS from the wrapper attribute.

Repository processing caches the effective theme through `workspace.fs`, so
remote workspaces need no synchronous filesystem fallback. A changed effective
theme or semantic contribution snapshot requests a preview refresh; unchanged
processing does not. Activation
awaits the initial repository snapshot before exposing the preview plugin.

```ts : <<renderCodeFence rule>>=


function previewThemeName(documents: SourceDocument[]): string {
  const theme = resolveProjectTheme(documents);
  return theme.diagnostics.length ? defaultThemeName : theme.name;
}

```

The document wrapper chooses its theme at render time, not parser installation
time. This lets a reused preview parser follow the document's workspace and its
latest open index buffer.

```ts : <<renderCodeFence rule>>=+
function installPreviewDocument(md: MarkdownIt, repository: FragmentRepository, navigation: PreviewNavigation): void {
  installScreenTermLinks(md);
  const sources = new WeakMap<Token[], string>();
  md.core.ruler.before('normalize', 'literate_preview_source', state => { sources.set(state.tokens, state.src); });
  const originalRender = md.renderer.render;
  md.renderer.render = function (tokens, options, env) {
    env = env || {};
    const snapshot = repository.getPreviewProject(env.currentDocument, sources.get(tokens));
    const destination = (target: string) => navigation.destination(env.currentDocument, snapshot, target);
    const literateScreen = { model: snapshot.publication, annotations: snapshot.annotations,
      path: snapshot.path, format: 'preview' as const, destination };
    const renderEnv = { ...env, literatePublication: snapshot.publication, literatePath: snapshot.path,
      literateTokens: snapshot.tokens, literateDestination: destination, literateScreen,
            literateUseTargets: screenTermUseAnchors(tokens, snapshot.annotations, snapshot.path) };
    const style = snapshot.annotations.terms.length || snapshot.annotations.notes.length ? `<style>${screenStyle}</style>` : '';
    const body = style + originalRender.call(this, tokens, options, renderEnv)
      + renderScreenIndexes(literateScreen, snapshot.paths);
    const theme = snapshot.theme;
    return `<div class="literate-document" data-literate-theme="${md.utils.escapeHtml(theme)}">${body}</div>`;
  };
}

```

Fence handling captures the previous rule separately from the document wrapper.
Settings are hidden and plain Mermaid is handed to the browser client; all other
fences proceed to the delegation and fragment-decoration path.

```ts : <<renderCodeFence rule>>=+
function installPreviewFences(md: MarkdownIt): void {
  const originalFence = md.renderer.rules.fence;
  md.renderer.rules.fence = (tokens, idx, options, env, slf) => {
    const token = tokens[idx];
    const context = env?.literateScreen;
    const annotation = context && screenIndex(context.model, context.annotations).annotationLines
      .get(context.path)?.get(token.map?.[0] ?? -1);
    if (annotation && annotation.body === token.content
      && env.literateTokens?.[annotation.tokenIndex]?.info === token.info) {
      return renderScreenAnnotation(annotation, context);
    }
    if (token.info.includes('SETTINGS')) { return ''; }
    if (token.info.trim() === 'mermaid') {
      const line = token.map ? ` data-line="${token.map[0]}"` : '';
      return `<pre class="literate-mermaid source-line"${line}>${md.utils.escapeHtml(token.content)}</pre>\n`;
    }
    <<delegate preview fences>>
  };
}
```

Ordinary fences delegate unchanged. Named fragments alone decode literal
opening delimiters (`\<<`) on a shallow token copy, leaving parser state intact.
A named Mermaid fragment is code, not a diagram: its fallback bypasses both
third-party fence rules and highlighters that might otherwise render Mermaid.

```ts : <<delegate preview fences>>=
const header = parseFragmentHeader(token.info);
if (!header) {
  return originalFence ? originalFence(tokens, idx, options, env, slf)
    : slf.renderToken(tokens, idx, options);
}
const previewToken = Object.assign(Object.create(Object.getPrototypeOf(token)), token);
previewToken.content = token.content.replace(/\\\<</g, '\<<');
previewToken.info = header.language;
const model = env.literatePublication as ReturnType<typeof parseProject>['publication'] | undefined;
const lookup = model && screenIndex(model);
const candidate = lookup?.lines.get(env.literatePath)?.get(token.map?.[0] ?? -1);
const part = candidate && candidate.content === token.content
  && candidate.kind === (header.append ? 'append' : 'definition')
  && lookup?.families.get(candidate.fragmentTarget)?.name === header.name && candidate.language === header.language
  && env.literateTokens?.[candidate.tokenIndex]?.info === token.info ? candidate : undefined;
const semantic = part && model ? renderScreenCode(part, model, 'preview', env.literateDestination) : undefined;
const fenceOptions = semantic !== undefined ? { ...options, highlight: () => semantic } : options;
const previewTokens = tokens.slice();
previewTokens[idx] = previewToken;
const rendered = header.language === 'mermaid'
  ? `<pre><code class="language-mermaid">${semantic ?? md.utils.escapeHtml(previewToken.content)}</code></pre>\n`
  : originalFence ? originalFence(previewTokens, idx, fenceOptions, env, slf)
    : `<pre><code>${semantic ?? md.utils.escapeHtml(previewToken.content)}</code></pre>\n`;
<<decorate preview fragments>>
```

Fragment labels and output filenames are text, not HTML. Escape them before
wrapping the code. Accepted contributions use shared semantic links and syntax
spans; stale or unaccepted tokens remain escaped code without invented targets.
Matching includes the complete authored header, not just its name and language:
a changed output path or invalid setting cannot borrow an older declaration.

```ts : <<decorate preview fragments>>=
const name = md.utils.escapeHtml(header.name);
const fileName = md.utils.escapeHtml(header.remainder.match(/^(.*?)\s+\$/)?.[1].trim() || '');
if (part && model) {
  const label = `\<<${header.name}>>=${header.append ? '+' : ''} ${header.remainder.match(/^(.*?)\s+\$/)?.[1].trim() || ''}`.trimEnd();
  return renderScreenContribution(part, model, 'preview', label, rendered, env.literateDestination);
}
return `<div class="codefragment">
<div class="fragmentname">&lt;&lt;${name}&gt;&gt;=${header.append ? '+' : ''} ${fileName}</div>
<div class="code">
${rendered}
</div>
</div>`;
```

## Capability-only preview navigation

Cross-document links enter through one activation-owned URI handler. Random
capabilities carry no filename, line or command. The bounded session map binds
workspace object identity and a logical target; removed workspaces cannot regain
old capabilities merely by reusing a URI. Untrusted workspaces mint nothing.

```ts : <<renderCodeFence rule>>=+
const previewUrlLifetime = 60_000;
type PreviewCapability = {
  folder: vscode.WorkspaceFolder; target: string; key: string;
  href?: string; expiresAt?: number; retryAt: number; resolving: boolean;
};
class PreviewNavigation implements vscode.UriHandler, vscode.Disposable {
  private readonly session = randomBytes(24).toString('hex');
  private readonly entries = new Map<string, PreviewCapability>();
  private readonly keys = new Map<string, string>();
  private readonly pending: string[] = [];
  private resolving = false;
  private disposed = false;
  private refresh: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
  private expiry: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
  private expiryAt = Infinity;
  private readonly membership = vscode.workspace.onDidChangeWorkspaceFolders(() => this.invalidateMembership());
  <<preview capability minting>>
  <<preview capability lookup>>
  <<preview capability resolution>>
  <<preview capability external resolution>>
  <<preview capability expiry scheduling>>
  <<preview capability expiry>>
  <<preview capability membership>>
  <<preview capability handling>>
  <<preview capability lifecycle>>
}
```

Only the renderer's accepted cross-document destinations reach this adapter.
Revalidate that target in its model before minting, and never substitute a native
line link while URL resolution is pending. The retained-entry limit avoids both
unbounded retention and eviction/remint/refresh loops on very large previews.
Removed workspaces release capacity without making their old tokens valid.

```ts : <<preview capability minting>>=
destination(document: vscode.Uri | undefined, model: Pick<ReturnType<typeof parseProject>, 'publication' | 'annotations'>, target: string) {
  const folder = document && vscode.workspace.getWorkspaceFolder(document);
  if (!folder || !this.allowed(folder) || !previewTarget(model, target)) { return undefined; }
  const key = JSON.stringify([folder.uri.toString(), target]);
  let token = this.keys.get(key);
  if (!token) {
    if (this.keys.size >= 4096) { return undefined; }
    token = this.session + '/' + randomBytes(24).toString('hex');
    this.keys.set(key, token);
    this.entries.set(token, { folder, target, key, retryAt: 0, resolving: false });
  }
  return this.resolvedDestination(token);
}
```

External URLs may become invalid without notification. Reuse a successful result
for at most one minute, then resolve the COMPLETE capability URI again on demand.
A pending request is shared by all renders; failures have a one-minute cooldown
and do not schedule automatic retries. Renewal never remints the capability.

```ts : <<preview capability lookup>>=
private resolvedDestination(token: string): string | undefined {
  const entry = this.entries.get(token);
  if (!entry) { return undefined; }
  const now = Date.now();
  if (entry.href && entry.expiresAt! > now) { return entry.href; }
  entry.href = undefined; entry.expiresAt = undefined;
  if (!entry.resolving && entry.retryAt <= now) {
    entry.resolving = true;
    this.pending.push(token);
    void this.resolve();
  }
  return undefined;
}
```

The resolver queue retains bounded parallelism for initial resolution and renewal.
Workspace removal prunes queued tokens; in-flight results still check their exact
entry before publishing, so an old response cannot populate a re-added workspace.

```ts : <<preview capability resolution>>=
private async resolve(): Promise<void> {
  if (this.resolving) { return; }
  this.resolving = true;
  try {
    while (!this.disposed && this.pending.length) {
      await Promise.all(this.pending.splice(0, 16).map(token => this.resolveExternal(token)));
    }
  } finally { this.resolving = false; }
}
```

The returned URI is opaque, including its window-routing query. Both initial
resolution and renewal store it verbatim. A failure clears the pending state but
cannot trigger a refresh/retry loop; later rendering may retry after the cooldown.

```ts : <<preview capability external resolution>>=
private async resolveExternal(token: string): Promise<void> {
  const entry = this.entries.get(token);
  if (!entry) { return; }
  try {
    if (!this.allowed(entry.folder)) { return; }
    const uri = vscode.Uri.parse(`${vscode.env.uriScheme}://jesterking.literate/navigate/${token}`);
    const external = await vscode.env.asExternalUri(uri);
    if (this.entries.get(token) === entry && this.allowed(entry.folder)) {
      entry.href = external.toString();
      entry.expiresAt = Date.now() + previewUrlLifetime;
      this.scheduleExpiry(entry.expiresAt);
      this.requestRefresh();
    }
  } catch (_) { /* Retry only on a later render after the cooldown. */ }
  finally { entry.resolving = false; entry.retryAt = entry.href ? 0 : Date.now() + previewUrlLifetime; }
}
```

One timer covers all successful URLs, regardless of the cache size. Publishing a
later expiry does not rescan the map or restart the earlier timer. Expiry requests
one coalesced refresh; only targets still rendered by the host are resolved again.

```ts : <<preview capability expiry scheduling>>=
private scheduleExpiry(at: number): void {
  if (this.disposed || at >= this.expiryAt) { return; }
  if (this.expiry !== undefined) { clearTimeout(this.expiry); }
  this.expiryAt = at;
  this.expiry = setTimeout(() => this.expireDestinations(), Math.max(0, at - Date.now()));
}
```

Expired URLs stop authorizing navigation while renewal is pending. Entries not
rendered again stay inert: they cannot start another timer or resolution. Failed
resolutions likewise have no expiry timer, only an on-demand retry timestamp.

```ts : <<preview capability expiry>>=
private expireDestinations(): void {
  this.expiry = undefined; this.expiryAt = Infinity;
  if (this.disposed) { return; }
  let changed = false;
  for (const entry of this.entries.values()) {
    if (entry.expiresAt === undefined) { continue; }
    if (entry.expiresAt <= Date.now()) {
      entry.href = undefined; entry.expiresAt = undefined; changed = true;
    } else { this.scheduleExpiry(entry.expiresAt); }
  }
  if (changed) { this.requestRefresh(); }
}
```

Removal invalidates both indexes and queued work. Re-adding the same URI can mint
new capabilities immediately, but old tokens and late resolver completions remain
invalid. Recompute the single expiry timer so removed workspaces leave no timer.

```ts : <<preview capability membership>>=
private invalidateMembership(): void {
  for (const [token, entry] of this.entries) {
    if (!this.allowed(entry.folder)) {
      this.entries.delete(token);
      this.keys.delete(entry.key);
    }
  }
  for (let index = this.pending.length - 1; index >= 0; index--) {
    if (!this.entries.has(this.pending[index])) { this.pending.splice(index, 1); }
  }
  if (this.expiry !== undefined) { clearTimeout(this.expiry); }
  this.expireDestinations();
}
```

Successful resolutions and expiry schedule refresh, coalesced outside rendering.
Disposal cancels both timers and invalidates in-flight results. No parser operation
or refresh command is awaited from a synchronous Markdown-it renderer.

```ts : <<preview capability lifecycle>>=
private allowed(folder: vscode.WorkspaceFolder): boolean {
  return !this.disposed && vscode.workspace.isTrusted === true
    && !!vscode.workspace.workspaceFolders?.includes(folder);
}
private requestRefresh(): void {
  if (this.refresh !== undefined) { return; }
  this.refresh = setTimeout(() => {
    this.refresh = undefined;
    if (!this.disposed) { void vscode.commands.executeCommand('markdown.preview.refresh').then(undefined, () => undefined); }
  }, 0);
}
dispose(): void {
  this.disposed = true;
  if (this.refresh !== undefined) { clearTimeout(this.refresh); }
  if (this.expiry !== undefined) { clearTimeout(this.expiry); }
  this.membership.dispose();
  this.entries.clear(); this.keys.clear(); this.pending.length = 0;
}
```

The incoming query is host-owned routing data, never an action or target. Exact
scheme, authority and path validation precedes map lookup; caller-supplied paths,
fragments, malformed tokens and capabilities from earlier sessions do nothing.

```ts : <<preview capability handling>>=
async handleUri(uri: vscode.Uri): Promise<void> {
  if (uri.scheme !== vscode.env.uriScheme || uri.authority !== 'jesterking.literate'
    || uri.fragment || !/^\/navigate\/[a-f0-9]{48}\/[a-f0-9]{48}$/.test(uri.path)) { return; }
  const token = uri.path.slice('/navigate/'.length);
  const entry = this.entries.get(token);
  const allowed = () => !!entry?.href && entry.expiresAt! > Date.now()
    && this.entries.get(token) === entry && this.allowed(entry.folder);
  if (!entry || !allowed()) { return; }
  try {
    await openPreviewTarget(entry, allowed);
  } catch (_) { /* Missing, unsafe or expired destinations never fall back to host links. */ }
}
```

Resolve logical identity against a fresh, read-only discovery, not the rendered
model or the repository's last debounce result. Keep a clean disk-only base even
for open files: otherwise discarding another buffer while opening the destination
would leave its former unsaved text in the final model. Missing disk files enter
only through current open buffers; other read failures abort navigation.

```ts : <<renderCodeFence rule>>=+
function previewTarget(model: Pick<ReturnType<typeof parseProject>, 'publication' | 'annotations'>, target: string) {
  return screenIndex(model.publication, model.annotations).targets.get(target);
}
async function previewNavigationSources(folder: vscode.WorkspaceFolder): Promise<Map<string, SourceDocument>> {
  const documents = new Map<string, SourceDocument>();
  for (const uri of await getLiterateFileUris(folder)) {
    const name = path.posix.relative(folder.uri.path, uri.path);
    if (safeRelativePath(name) !== name || uri.toString() !== vscode.Uri.joinPath(folder.uri, name).toString()) { continue; }
    try {
      const text = new TextDecoder('utf-8').decode(await vscode.workspace.fs.readFile(uri));
      documents.set(name, { path: name, text });
    } catch (error) {
      if (!(error instanceof vscode.FileSystemError) || error.code !== 'FileNotFound') { throw error; }
    }
  }
  return documents;
}
```

Canonical URI equality prevents a fragment-qualified host document from becoming
an alternate source of truth. Each overlay copies the clean disk base and adds
only currently open buffers, so closing/discarding a buffer restores disk contents
(or removes an unsaved-only input). Excluded directories follow ordinary discovery.

```ts : <<renderCodeFence rule>>=+
function overlayNavigationSources(folder: vscode.WorkspaceFolder, disk: ReadonlyMap<string, SourceDocument>) {
  const documents = new Map(disk);
  for (const doc of vscode.workspace.textDocuments) {
    const name = path.posix.relative(folder.uri.path, doc.uri.path);
    if (!doc.isClosed && name.endsWith('.literate') && safeRelativePath(name) === name
      && !name.split('/').slice(0, -1).some(value => discoveryExcludedDirectories.includes(value))
      && doc.uri.toString() === vscode.Uri.joinPath(folder.uri, name).toString()
      && vscode.workspace.getWorkspaceFolder(doc.uri) === folder) {
      documents.set(name, { path: name, text: doc.getText() });
    }
  }
  return [...documents.values()];
}
```

Inspect every component beneath the workspace boundary before opening. Symlinks
and non-files fail closed rather than escaping that boundary. A deleted file is
not recreated even if its former buffer remains open.

```ts : <<renderCodeFence rule>>=+
async function safePreviewDocument(folder: vscode.WorkspaceFolder, name: string): Promise<vscode.Uri> {
  if (safeRelativePath(name) !== name || !name.endsWith('.literate')) { throw new Error('Unsafe preview target'); }
  const parts = name.split('/');
  let uri = folder.uri;
  for (let index = 0; index < parts.length; index++) {
    uri = vscode.Uri.joinPath(uri, parts[index]);
    const stat = await vscode.workspace.fs.stat(uri);
    const wanted = index === parts.length - 1 ? vscode.FileType.File : vscode.FileType.Directory;
    if (stat.type !== wanted) { throw new Error('Unsafe preview target component'); }
  }
  if (vscode.workspace.getWorkspaceFolder(uri) !== folder) { throw new Error('Preview workspace changed'); }
  return uri;
}
```

Opening may await a provider. Re-overlay buffers and resolve once more afterwards,
then select only if logical identity still addresses that same canonical document.
The final checks and selection calculation have no intervening asynchronous work.

```ts : <<renderCodeFence rule>>=+
async function openPreviewTarget(entry: PreviewCapability, allowed: () => boolean): Promise<void> {
  const inputs = await previewNavigationSources(entry.folder);
  const part = previewTarget(parseProject(overlayNavigationSources(entry.folder, inputs)), entry.target);
  if (!part || !allowed()) { return; }
  const uri = await safePreviewDocument(entry.folder, part.definition.path);
  if (!allowed()) { return; }
  const document = await vscode.workspace.openTextDocument(uri);
  await safePreviewDocument(entry.folder, part.definition.path);
  const current = overlayNavigationSources(entry.folder, inputs);
  const fresh = previewTarget(parseProject(current), entry.target);
  if (!allowed() || document.isClosed || document.uri.toString() !== uri.toString()
    || !fresh || fresh.definition.path !== part.definition.path) { return; }
  const position = new vscode.Position(fresh.definition.line, 0);
  await vscode.window.showTextDocument(document, { selection: new vscode.Range(position, position) });
}
```

## Register the literate.process command

The command `literate.process` is registered with Visual Studio Code. The
disposable that gets returned by `registerCommand` is held in
`literateProcessDisposable` so that we can push it into `context.subscriptions`.

Here we find the main program of our `literate.process` command. Our
*MarkdownIt* is set up, `.literate` files are searched and iterated. Each
`.literate` file is rendered, and code fragments are harvested. Finally code
fragments are extrapolated and saved to their respective source code files. The
`HTML` files are also saved to files.

Diagnostic messages are also handled here. Errors and warnings are shown where
necessary. On successfull completion a simple status bar message will be used.
An information diagnostic message is not good here, because that will prevent
the usage of `literate.process` in for instance `tasks.json`, since the
diagnostic message will block execution of a task if it were used as prelaunch
task. That is obviously not good for the workflow.

```ts : <<register literate.process>>=
let literateProcessDisposable = vscode.commands.registerCommand(
  'literate.process',
  async function () {
    await theOneRepository.processLiterateFiles(undefined, true);
    return vscode.window.setStatusBarMessage("Literate Process completed", 5000);
});

context.subscriptions.push(literateProcessDisposable);
```

## Register the literate.create_fragment_for_tag command

``` ts : <<register literate.create_fragment_for_tag>>=
let literateCreateFragmentForTagDisposable = vscode.commands.registerCommand(
  'literate.create_fragment_for_tag',
  async function (range? : vscode.Range) {
    createFragmentForTag(range);
  }
);

context.subscriptions.push(literateCreateFragmentForTagDisposable);
```

### literate.create_fragment_for_tag implementation

The `literate.create_fragment_for_tag` will do as its name suggests. When the
position in the document is on a tag then the command will add a code fence to
the document.

If the tag at the position is fragment usage in a fragment then the code fence
will be created after the current fragment using the same language as specified
in the current fragment. If the tag is a fragment mention then instead the
current fragment map is checked for what the most used language is and that is
pre-filled.

First we ensure we have an active editor.

``` ts : <<create fragment for tag>>=
function createFragmentForTag(range? : vscode.Range)
{
  let activeEditor = vscode.window.activeTextEditor;
  if(activeEditor)
  {
```

From the active editor we find the document. The editor also has the information
about where our cursor currently is, under `active` property on `selection`, but
we use `range.start` if `range` was passed in.

``` ts : <<create fragment for tag>>=+
    const document = activeEditor.document;
    const position = range ? range.start : activeEditor.selection.active;
```

For the document and position we determined we can get the fragment at that
location. We retrieve that from the repository so that we can get the range for
the fragment use.

``` ts : <<create fragment for tag>>=+
    const fragmentLocation = theOneRepository.getFragmentTagLocation(
      document,
      document.lineAt(position),
      position);
```

With the range of the fragment use in hand we can find the **Markdown** token
where this range is contained for the document we're in.

``` ts : <<create fragment for tag>>=+
    const tokenUsage = theOneRepository.getTokenAtPosition(
      document,
      fragmentLocation.range);
```

The token needs to be valid to be able to use it for determining the insert. The
`map` property on `Token` will tell us the begin and end lines. We want to add
our new fragment definition after this token. We'll access the `map` when we are
ready to do an `insert` on the `WorkspaceEdit`.

We initialize a temporary language id to `LANGID`.

``` ts : <<create fragment for tag>>=+
    if(tokenUsage.token && tokenUsage.token.map)
    {
      let workspaceEdit = new vscode.WorkspaceEdit();
      let langId : string = 'LANGID';
```

If we have a `fence` `Token` we try matching the `info` property of the token
with `parseFragmentHeader`. This gives us the language id used for that fence. We'll be
using the same language id for the new code fragment.

``` ts : <<create fragment for tag>>=+
      if(tokenUsage.token.type === 'fence' && tokenUsage.token.map)
      {
        const header = parseFragmentHeader(tokenUsage.token.info);
        if(header) {
          langId = header.language;
        }
      }
```

We can now create the new fragment string with the language id and the fragment
tag name we want to create the fragment for.

Newlines at the begin and end of the string ensure the fragment won't be created
without the necessary empty lines.

``` ts : <<create fragment for tag>>=+
      let newFragment = `\n${FENCE} ${langId} : ${OPENING}${fragmentLocation.name}${CLOSING}=\n${FENCE}\n`;
```

Now that we have the new fragment text ready we can call `insert` on our
workspace edit. The position is created with the second element of the
`token.map` as the line number, and 0 to have the insert happen at the beginning
of the line.

Finally we apply the workspace edit to our workspace. This will give us the new
fragment right after the paragraph or code fence with the fragment name we found
at the position we ran the command at.

``` ts : <<create fragment for tag>>=+
      workspaceEdit.insert(
        document.uri,
        new vscode.Position(tokenUsage.token.map[1], 0),
        newFragment
        );
      vscode.workspace.applyEdit(workspaceEdit);
    }
  }
}
```

## Register the literate.split_fragment command

Registering the `literate.split_fragment` command, setting it up so that it
could take a `vscode.Position` parameter, which helps in programmatically firing
the command for a certain pre-computed location.

``` ts : <<register literate.split_fragment>>=
let literateSplitFragmentDisposable = vscode.commands.registerCommand(
  'literate.split_fragment',
  async function (position? : vscode.Position) {
    splitFragment(position);
  }
);

context.subscriptions.push(literateSplitFragmentDisposable);
```

### literate.split_fragment implementation

The `literate.split_fragment` will split the current fragment below the line
where the cursor is. If no active text editor was found nothing will happen.
With one in hand though we can either use the position given to the method, or
otherwise use the cursor location in the document.

```ts : <<split fragment>>=
function splitFragment(position_? : vscode.Position)
{
  let activeEditor = vscode.window.activeTextEditor;
  if(activeEditor)
  {
    const document = activeEditor.document;
    const position = position_ ? position_ : activeEditor.selection.active;
```

With the document and position we can find the `Token` at that location.
Continue only if it is a fence.

```ts : <<split fragment>>=+
    const tokenUsage = theOneRepository.getTokenAtPosition(
      document,
      new vscode.Range(position, position));
    if(tokenUsage.token && tokenUsage.token.type === 'fence')
    {
```

Next we match the info line, we want to ensure we have actually a fragment here.

```ts : <<split fragment>>=+
      const header = parseFragmentHeader(tokenUsage.token.info);
      if(header)
      {
```

From the matched info line we take the language identifier and the fragment tag
name. We can create the text that will split the current fragment.

```ts : <<split fragment>>=+
        let langId = header.language;
        let tagName = header.name.trim();
        let textToInsert = `${FENCE}\n\n${FENCE}${langId} : ${OPENING}${tagName}${CLOSING}=+\n`;
```

Finally we can create the workspace edit, make the insert on the next line from
the cursor, and apply the edit.

```ts : <<split fragment>>=+
        let workspaceEdit = new vscode.WorkspaceEdit();
        workspaceEdit.insert(
          document.uri,
          new vscode.Position(position.line+1, 0),
          textToInsert
          );
        vscode.workspace.applyEdit(workspaceEdit);
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Diagnostics

Generation diagnostics come exclusively from the engine, including malformed
headers, missing templates, invalid output paths, missing fragments and cycles.
The adapter preserves their zero-based line and column coordinates, grouping
errors by full document URI in the Problems panel. Navigation helpers do not
emit a second, potentially contradictory set of validation errors.

## Utility functions

### Discovering inputs and reading buffers

Discovery includes all `.literate` files, not just index-linked chapters. Passing
`null` as the exclusion argument avoids silently dropping files through editor
search exclusions. Both discovered URIs and open buffers are filtered using the
engine's shared `discoveryExcludedDirectories`, matching CLI discovery. Exact
directory components relative to the workspace root are checked at every depth:
`node_modules`, `out`, `dist`, `.git`, `.yarn`, and `.vscode-test` are excluded.
Similar names and filenames are not excluded. Open workspace documents are
merged by full URI so new,
not-yet-saved file-backed documents participate too. Untitled documents have no
workspace-relative output location and are not included. Discovery sorts full
URIs; the engine applies the semantic chapter ordering after contents are read.

The buffer lookup uses full URI equality, not relative names that can collide
between workspace folders. Presence, rather than truthiness of the text,
decides whether to read from disk: an empty buffer must stay empty. This same
lookup applies to templates, so unsaved template edits are used on the next
processing request. Automatic processing watches literate content changes only;
use Process after changing a template without changing a literate document.

``` ts : <<utility functions>>=
async function getLiterateFileUris(workspaceFolder: vscode.WorkspaceFolder): Promise<vscode.Uri[]> {
  const uris = new Map<string, vscode.Uri>();
  const excluded = new Set<string>(discoveryExcludedDirectories);
  const included = (uri: vscode.Uri): boolean => !path.posix
    .relative(workspaceFolder.uri.path, uri.path).split('/').slice(0, -1)
    .some(directory => excluded.has(directory));
  const pattern = new vscode.RelativePattern(workspaceFolder, '**/*.literate');
  for (const uri of await vscode.workspace.findFiles(pattern, null)) {
    if (included(uri)) { uris.set(uri.toString(), uri); }
  }
  for (const document of vscode.workspace.textDocuments) {
    if (document.uri.path.endsWith('.literate') && included(document.uri) &&
        vscode.workspace.getWorkspaceFolder(document.uri)?.uri.toString() === workspaceFolder.uri.toString()) {
      uris.set(document.uri.toString(), document.uri);
    }
  }
  return Array.from(uris.keys()).sort().map(key => uris.get(key)!);
}

```

Discovery determines membership; reading determines which version enters the
snapshot. A matching editor buffer wins even when it is empty, avoiding a silent
fallback to stale disk content for either documents or templates.

``` ts : <<utility functions>>=+
async function getFileContent(file: vscode.Uri): Promise<string> {
  const document = vscode.workspace.textDocuments.find(
    document => document.uri.toString() === file.toString());
  if (document !== undefined) { return document.getText(); }
  return new TextDecoder('utf-8').decode(await vscode.workspace.fs.readFile(file));
}
```

## The extension

Our Visual Studio Code entry file is the `extension.ts` file. While developing
the plug-in the JavaScript version created from this, in `out/extension.js` is
set as the entry point for the extension, in `package.json`. But when it is
prepared for release on the Visual Studio Code marketplace this needs to be
changed to the minified and bundled version that gets realized as `out/main.js`.
This ensures, together with a properly set up `.vscodeignore` that the published
package stays small in size. Without that the package is easily over 2MB in
size, but properly configured it is under 400KB.

The extension main entry lies in the activation of the extension, as given by
`<<activate the extension>>`, but before we get there we need to set up several
bits and pieces that are required for the proper functioning of the tools.

First of all we import all the functionality and modules we are going to need.

``` ts : <<literate.*>>= ./src/extension.ts $ template=tssrc.template
/* Literate Programming by Nathan 'jesterKing' Letwory */

<<import necessary modules for literate>>
```

After the imports we introduce the fence delimiters used by editor commands.
Each preview parser keeps its original fence renderer in its own installation
closure, so creating an internal parser cannot change another parser's rule.

HTML settings belong to the engine invocation, not global extension state, so
settings cannot leak from one workspace project to another.

```ts : <<literate.*>>=+

const FENCE = '```';
const OPENING = '\<<';
const CLOSING = '>>';

```

With that out of the way we introduce the interfaces we use in the Literate
Programming extension.

```ts : <<literate.*>>=+
<<introduce interfaces>>

```

Next we define everything needed to implement the fragment explorer. This explorer will show up in the Explorer bar
when a literate project is open. We need a representation for a node in the tree
view, a data provider for the tree view and then the actual tree view explorer
itself.

```ts : <<literate.*>>=+

<<fragment node>>

<<fragment tree provider>>

<<fragment explorer>>

<<fragment hover provider>>
```

For our extension we need to override the code fence rule since we want to
augment the rendering of the code fences. Specifically we want to add the
fragment line prior to the code block. This is explained in the section on
`<<renderCodeFence rule>>`.

Also we have a way to create a *MarkdownIt* parser the way we need it. It is
explained in more detail in the section on `<<create markdownit parser>>`.

``` ts : <<literate.*>>=+
<<renderCodeFence rule>>

<<create markdownit parser>>

```

The repository bridges editor state and the pure engine. Unexpanded fragments
remain available for navigation without duplicating output generation.

``` ts : <<literate.*>>=+
<<handle fragments>>
<<fragment repository>>
<<rename provider class>>
<<code action provider class>>
<<definition provider class>>
<<reference provider class>>
```

Utility function to determine the workspace folder for a TextDocument

``` ts : <<literate.*>>=+
function determineWorkspaceFolder(document : vscode.TextDocument) : vscode.WorkspaceFolder | undefined
{
  return vscode.workspace.getWorkspaceFolder(document.uri);
}
```

Although the fragments mentioned above are the soul of the extension they are
not of much use without the proper activation. With this `activate`
implementation all providers and commands are registered with Visual Studio
Code.

``` ts : <<literate.*>>=+
<<activate the extension>>
<<utility functions>>
<<create fragment for tag>>
<<split fragment>>
```

There is nothing currently needed for deactivation of the extension, so there is
just an empty-bodied implementation for it.

``` ts : <<literate.*>>=+
export function deactivate() {}
```

